The code folder that you have open serves is for a custom PCB with a teensy that sends CAN commands to an ESC that is running MESC firmware. The basis of the positional control will be to use torque commands. I have a MT6701 encoder gathering data, and the ESC sends velocity and position by CAN to the teensy. The frequency of the updates are 500hz.

Implementation of the teensy code involved here includes entering into the control loop when the user presses a button, and at that point we'll loop into position_control_mode(). We can control how long it is that mode with following the variables "elapsed" and "sup->user_total_us". The code will then send telemetry data at that point.

----------------------------------------
Basic parameters:
----------------------------------------
Units:
- sup->esc[0].state.pos_rad: radians
- sup->esc[0].state.vel_rad_s: radians/second
- position, velocity in position mode inherit those units (position_control_mode.cpp (line 110), position_control_mode.cpp (line 111))
- dt is seconds
- vel_filt_wheel: radians/second
- WHEEL_RADIUS_M: meters
- IMU quaternion pitch sup->imu.pitch_rad: radians (supervisor.h (line 73), supervisor.cpp (line 399), supervisor.cpp (line 414))
- CONTROL_PERIOD_US: microseconds (supervisor.h (line 20))
- sup->user_setpoint: radians
- microseconds 
  - sup->timing.dt_us
  - exec_time_us
  - last_tick_us
  - elapsed, start_time
  - sup->user_total_us

Safety envelop: 
- clamp the amperage that is sent by CAN to be between +TORQUE_CLAMP and -TORQUE_CLAMP
- when the timer variable, elapsed, has exceeded sup->user_total_us send CAN torque = 0

Protocols: 
- You should be able to determine the format for sending and receiving CAN commands from the existing code
- This enables you to send torque command
- Receive velocity, position

----------------------------------------
Control theory: 
----------------------------------------

The position controller will be a torque-mode outer loop running at 500 Hz.

The system may exhibit static friction.

The controller is allowed to introduce a small breakaway torque when |position error| > threshold and velocity ≈ 0.

Plant interface:
- Input: torque command sent over CAN
- Output: position and velocity received over CAN

Controller structure (initial implementation):
- Position error: e = wrap_to_pi(sup->user_setpoint - pos_rad)
- Velocity feedback: use sup->esc[0].state.vel_rad_s (or vel_filt_wheel if specified in code)
- Control law (PD + optional feedforward):

  tau_unsat =
      Kp * e
    + Kd * (vel_setpoint - vel_measured)
    + tau_ff

Where:
- vel_setpoint = 0 for position hold
- tau_ff may include:
  - static friction compensation (sign(e) * tau_coulomb)
  - viscous term (Kv * vel_measured)
  - gravity term (future, not required now)

Derivative term:
- Must be implemented using measured velocity, not finite difference of position.
- A first-order low-pass filter may be applied to the derivative term.

Saturation:
- tau_cmd = clamp(tau_unsat, -TORQUE_CLAMP, +TORQUE_CLAMP)

Slew rate limit:
- Optional: limit |Δtau/Δt|

Angle wrap:
- All position errors must use shortest-path wrap in radians.

Timing:
- Fixed sample time dt = CONTROL_PERIOD_US * 1e-6

----------------------------------------
Test parameters
----------------------------------------
Standard test during position_control_mode():

Step test:
- Initial position is the current measured position.
- Apply a step in sup->user_setpoint.
- Run until elapsed >= sup->user_total_us.

Performance objectives:
- Minimize settling time to |position error| < 0.02 rad
- Overshoot < 10%
- Steady-state error < 0.005 rad
- Avoid sustained oscillation
- Minimize time spent in torque saturation

The controller should be tuned by adjusting Kp, Kd, derivative filter, and feedforward terms.

----------------------------------------
Telemetry output:
----------------------------------------

The following internal variables must be logged:

- position error (after wrap)
- tau_unsat
- tau_cmd
- saturation flag
- loop execution time (exec_time_us)
- dt_s

These are required for controller evaluation.

During position_control_mode(), buffer telemetry and transmit after the control interval.

The telemetry must be emitted as JSON with the following structure:

{
  "log_version": 1,
  "sample_rate_hz": 500,
  "test_type": "position_step",
  "setpoint_rad": <float>,
  "duration_us": <uint32>,
  "data": [
    {
      "t_s": <float>,
      "pos_rad": <float>,
      "vel_rad_s": <float>,
      "pos_error_rad": <float>,
      "tau_cmd": <float>,
      "tau_unsat": <float>,
      "saturated": <0|1>,
      "dt_s": <float>
    }
  ]
}

Requirements:
- JSON must be valid and parseable by Python without modification.
- Field names must remain unchanged once established.
- All numeric values must be in SI units.

----------------------------------------
Allowed changes: 
----------------------------------------

You can change:
- PD gains, derivative filter, feedforward terms
- error wrap handling
- torque clamps/slew
- motion profiling (trapezoid / S-curve) if you want

You are not allowed to change: 
- CAN protocol IDs/packing (unless you explicitly permit)
- button/entry logic structure
- telemetry schema once established (version it instead)

Control loop timing:
- The control loop runs at a fixed period defined by CONTROL_PERIOD_US.
- The controller must not block or perform dynamic memory allocation.
- Execution time must remain less than CONTROL_PERIOD_US.